The loop itself. =train N { }= binds =step= and collects each iteration's
final value into =last_losses=. Each step selects one name's row, runs one
=adam= (forward, backward and update of all nine matrices, about 0.6 ms),
and writes Python's progress line, carriage return included. Its output is
not kept here; the next block summarizes it.

#+begin_src mlpl :results silent
train num_steps {
  row = take(train_tokens, 0, step);
  n = at(train_n, step);
  inp = u:doc_in(row, n);
  tgt = u:doc_tgt(row, n);
  mask = u:causal_mask(n);
  lr_t = learning_rate * (1 - step / num_steps);
  loss = adam(u:loss(inp, tgt, mask), [wte, wpe, lm_head, layer0_attn_wq, layer0_attn_wk, layer0_attn_wv, layer0_attn_wo, layer0_mlp_fc1, layer0_mlp_fc2], lr_t, beta1, beta2, eps_adam);
